{"summary": "S2 claims, S3 book questions (the book's own solutions, typed for marking, verified three ways), S4 visuals from existing kinds, S8 coverage oracle — per lesson, after G1", "agentCount": 8, "logs": ["1 lesson(s): g10m2s3-1 (20 items, 2 WE, 0 figures)"], "result": {"stage": "S2-S4,S8", "workflow": "lesson", "prompts_version": "lesson-v8", "collect_version": "collect-6", "book": "g10-math", "embedded": {"source": "runbook/lesson.workflow.js", "source_sha256": "c79e2c6ea317ffec161f87ed2dd081980264f4d7ff6517d88966e671b3db0eb5", "args_sha256": "43890e4f8adf351b9de7a56ae942b78c0b251a04bcaaa5b8032bb80c80839946", "generated_sha256": "480ab26591042f5eee6e43cc2a1b7d238887b1ff4f6afa146500ea9bca11694a", "generator": "services/extraction/embed_workflow.py v1"}, "lessons": [{"lesson": "g10m2s3-1", "claims": [{"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "rule", "text": "The exponent laws can also be applied to expressions with rational exponents.", "anchor": "b00884", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "We can also apply the exponent laws to expressions with rational exponents.", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "To simplify a product of powers of the same base with rational exponents, add the exponents: in 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2) the powers of x combine as x^(1/2 - 1/2).", "anchor": "WE6", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "8{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "When simplifying 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2), the coefficients 2 and 4 are multiplied to give 8.", "anchor": "WE6", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "4{x}^{-\\frac{1}{2}}&=8", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "Adding the exponents 1/2 and -1/2 gives the exponent 0, so 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2) becomes 8x^0.", "anchor": "WE6", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "8{x}^{0}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "A power with exponent 0 is replaced by 1, so 8x^0 = 8(1) = 8.", "anchor": "WE6", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "8\\left(1\\right)", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "method", "text": "To simplify a decimal base raised to a rational exponent, write the base as a fraction and simplify it.", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "Write as a fraction and simplify:", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "method", "text": "The decimal 0,008 is written as the fraction 8/1000, so (0,008)^(1/3) = (8/1000)^(1/3).", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "\\frac{8}{1000}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "method", "text": "The fraction 8/1000 is simplified to 1/125, so (8/1000)^(1/3) = (1/125)^(1/3).", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "\\frac{1}{125}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "A rational exponent on a fraction is applied to the numerator and to the denominator separately: (1/5^3)^(1/3) = 1^(1/3) / 5^(3 · 1/3).", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "\\frac{1^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{5^{\\left(3\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "type": "method", "text": "A power raised to a rational exponent has its exponents multiplied: (5^3)^(1/3) = 5^(3 · 1/3).", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "5^{\\left(3\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "method", "text": "Evaluating by this method gives (0,008)^(1/3) = 1/5.", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "\\frac{1}{5}", "provenance": "containment"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "convention", "text": "The book writes a decimal number with a comma as the decimal separator, for example 0,008.", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "0,008", "provenance": "checked"}, {"lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "type": "method", "text": "The denominator 125 is rewritten as the prime power 5^3, so (1/125)^(1/3) = (1/5^3)^(1/3).", "anchor": "WE7", "printed_page": 50, "supported": true, "teacher_only": false, "quote": "5^{3}", "provenance": "checked"}], "claims_dropped": [], "items": [{"ref": "WE6", "kind": "worked_example", "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1", "stem": "Simplify: $2{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\times4{x}^{-\\frac{1}{2}}$", "answer_type": "numeric", "answer": "8", "choices": null, "marker": null, "solution": ["$\\begin{align*}2{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\times4{x}^{-\\frac{1}{2}}&=8{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}}\\\\&=8{x}^{0}\\\\&=8\\left(1\\right)\\\\&=8\\end{align*}$"], "solution_provenance": "book_worked", "printed_answer": null, "epub_final_answer": "8", "blind_answer": "8", "verification": "agreed", "tier": "standard", "printed_page": 50, "shortcode": null, "verify": {"pairs": [{"pair_id": "WE6|blind~book", "route": "normalised", "verdict": "equivalent"}]}, "typing_problems": [], "unit": null, "options_source": null, "not_markable_reason": null, "tier_source": "typing", "printed_answer_scope": null, "figures": [], "figures_missing": [], "form_overridden": null, "form_unsupported": null, "blocked_on_figure": false}, {"ref": "WE7", "kind": "worked_example", "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2", "stem": "Simplify: ${\\left(\\text{0,008}\\right)}^{\\frac{1}{3}}$", "answer_type": "expression", "answer": "\\frac{1}{5}", "choices": null, "marker": {"kind": "expression", "key": "\\frac{1}{5}", "form": null, "variables": [], "tolerance": null}, "solution": ["Write as a fraction and simplify: $